WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:03.133
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:08.494
Give me one minute,
and I'll show you how Bigtable

00:00:08.494 --> 00:00:10.823
can help you analyze large workloads.

00:00:10.824 --> 00:00:15.023
Cloud Bigtable is a fully managed,
scalable NoSQL database

00:00:15.024 --> 00:00:17.655
for large analytical
and operational workloads.

00:00:17.988 --> 00:00:20.608
It is designed
as a sparsely populated table

00:00:20.888 --> 00:00:24.588
that can scale to billions of rows
and thousands of columns,

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:29.022
enabling you to store terabytes
or even petabytes of data.

00:00:29.822 --> 00:00:31.789
Bigtable is a key-value store

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:34.012
that supports high read
and write throughput

00:00:34.012 --> 00:00:35.208
at low latency,

00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:37.462
supporting millions
of requests per second.

00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:40.203
Need to increase queries per second?

00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:42.122
Just add more Bigtable nodes.

00:00:43.088 --> 00:00:46.783
It is an ideal data source
for MapReduce-style operations

00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:49.506
and integrates easily
with existing big data tools,

00:00:49.506 --> 00:00:51.882
like Hadoop, Dataflow, and Dataproc.

00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:53.889
To help you get started quickly,

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:57.158
it also supports
open-source HBase API standard.

00:00:57.755 --> 00:00:59.348
Ready to set up Bigtable?

00:00:59.722 --> 00:01:01.788
Get started by designing the schema,

00:01:02.088 --> 00:01:04.922
planning out queries,
and designing a row key.

00:01:05.222 --> 00:01:07.789
Once finalized, create a Bigtable instance

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.712
using this <i>gcloud</i> command,
Cloud Console, or the API.

00:01:12.622 --> 00:01:14.523
In Bigtable, you write data once,

00:01:14.524 --> 00:01:18.222
and it automatically replicates
where needed with eventual consistency,

00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:20.389
giving you control for high availability

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.538
and isolation of read and write workloads.

00:01:23.355 --> 00:01:26.423
Bigtable powers Google products
used by billions,

00:01:26.424 --> 00:01:28.255
such as Search and Maps.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:31.388
You can use it to store
and query your data, too,

00:01:31.388 --> 00:01:35.075
including time series,
marketing, and financial data.

00:01:35.588 --> 00:01:38.678
So, next time you're looking
to analyze large workloads,

00:01:39.022 --> 00:01:40.988
take a minute to look at Bigtable.

00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:44.855
To learn more, check out
<i>cloud.google.com/bigtable.</i>

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.992
♪ [music] ♪

